1 OCCU Professor, Nobelpristagaren i kemi 1903
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Svante_Arrhenius '''Svante August Arrhenius'''] (19 February 1859 – 2 October 1927) was a Swedish scientist. Originally a physicist, but often referred to as a chemist, Arrhenius was 
2 CONC one of the founders of the science of physical chemistry. He received the Nobel Prize for Chemistry in 1903, becoming the first Swedish Nobel laureate. In 1905, he became director of the Nobel Institute, where he remained until hi
2 CONC s death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrhenius was the first to use basic principles of physical chemistry to estimate the extent to which increases in atmospheric carbon dioxide are responsible for the Earth's increasing surface temperature. In the 1960s, David Keel
2 CONC ing demonstrated that human-caused carbon dioxide emissions are large enough to cause global warming.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrhenius' contributions to science are memorialized by the Arrhenius equation, Arrhenius acid, lunar crater Arrhenius, Martian crater Arrhenius, the mountain of Arrheniusfjellet, and the Arrhenius Labs at Stockholm University. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early years'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrhenius was born on 19 February 1859 at Vik (also spelled Wik or Wijk), near Uppsala, Kingdom of Sweden, United Kingdoms of Sweden and Norway, the son of Svante Gustav and Carolina Thunberg Arrhenius. His father had been a land 
2 CONC surveyor for Uppsala University, moving up to a supervisory position. At the age of three, Arrhenius taught himself to read without the encouragement of his parents, and by watching his father's addition of numbers in his account 
2 CONC books, became an arithmetical prodigy. In later life, Arrhenius was profoundly passionate about mathematical concepts, data analysis and discovering their relationships and laws.
2 CONT
2 CONT At age eight, he entered the local cathedral school, starting in the fifth grade, distinguishing himself in physics and mathematics, and graduating as the youngest and most able student in 1876.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ionic disassociation''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the University of Uppsala, he was dissatisfied with the chief instructor of physics and the only faculty member who could have supervised him in chemistry, Per Teodor Cleve, so he left to study at the Physical Institute of the 
2 CONC Swedish Academy of Sciences in Stockholm under the physicist Erik Edlund in 1881.
2 CONT
2 CONT His work focused on the conductivities of electrolytes. In 1884, based on this work, he submitted a 150-page dissertation on electrolytic conductivity to Uppsala for the doctorate. It did not impress the professors, among whom was
2 CONC  Cleve, and he received a fourth-class degree, but upon his defense it was reclassified as third-class. Later, extensions of this very work would earn him the 1903 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrhenius put forth 56 theses in his 1884 dissertation, most of which would still be accepted today unchanged or with minor modifications. The most important idea in the dissertation was his explanation of the fact that solid crys
2 CONC talline salts disassociate into paired charged particles when dissolved, for which he would win the 1903 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Arrhenius's explanation was that in forming a solution, the salt disassociates into charged particl
2 CONC es, to which Michael Faraday had given the name ions many years earlier. Faraday's belief had been that ions were produced in the process of electrolysis, that is, an external direct current source of electricity was necessary to 
2 CONC form ions. Arrhenius proposed that, even in the absence of an electric current, aqueous solutions of salts contained ions. He thus proposed that chemical reactions in solution were reactions between ions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The dissertation did not impress the professors at Uppsala, but Arrhenius sent it to a number of scientists in Europe who were developing the new science of physical chemistry, such as Rudolf Clausius, Wilhelm Ostwald, and J. H. v
2 CONC an 't Hoff. They were far more impressed, and Ostwald even came to Uppsala to persuade Arrhenius to join his research team. Arrhenius declined, however, as he preferred to stay in Sweden-Norway for a while (his father was very ill
2 CONC  and would die in 1885) and had received an appointment at Uppsala.
2 CONT
2 CONT In an extension of his ionic theory Arrhenius proposed definitions for acids and bases, in 1884. He believed that acids were substances that produce hydrogen ions in solution and that bases were substances that produce hydroxide i
2 CONC ons in solution.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Middle period''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1885 Arrhenius next received a travel grant from the Swedish Academy of Sciences, which enabled him to study with Ostwald in Riga (now in Latvia), with Friedrich Kohlrausch in Würzburg, Germany, with Ludwig Boltzmann in Graz, A
2 CONC ustria, and with van 't Hoff in Amsterdam.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1889 Arrhenius explained the fact that most reactions require added heat energy to proceed by formulating the concept of activation energy, an energy barrier that must be overcome before two molecules will react. The Arrhenius 
2 CONC equation gives the quantitative basis of the relationship between the activation energy and the rate at which a reaction proceeds.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1891 he became a lecturer at the Stockholm University College (Stockholms Högskola, now Stockholm University), being promoted to professor of physics (with much opposition) in 1895, and rector in 1896.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Nobel Prizes''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1900, Arrhenius became involved in setting up the Nobel Institutes and the Nobel Prizes. He was elected a member of the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences in 1901. For the rest of his life, he would be a member of the Nobel C
2 CONC ommittee on Physics and a de facto member of the Nobel Committee on Chemistry. He used his positions to arrange prizes for his friends (Jacobus van't Hoff, Wilhelm Ostwald, Theodore Richards) and to attempt to deny them to his ene
2 CONC mies (Paul Ehrlich, Walther Nernst, Dmitri Mendeleev). In 1901 Arrhenius was elected to the Swedish Academy of Sciences, against strong opposition. In 1903 he became the first Swede to be awarded the Nobel Prize in chemistry. In 1
2 CONC 905, upon the founding of the Nobel Institute for Physical Research at Stockholm, he was appointed rector of the institute, the position where he remained until retirement in 1927.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1911 he won the first Willard Gibbs Award.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Society memberships'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected an Honorary Member of the Netherlands Chemical Society in 1909.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became a Foreign Member of the Royal Society (ForMemRS) in 1910.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1912, he was elected a Foreign Honorary Member of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1919 he became foreign member of the Royal Netherlands Academy of Arts and Sciences.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Later years''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, Arrhenius's theories became generally accepted and he turned to other scientific topics. In 1902 he began to investigate physiological problems in terms of chemical theory. He determined that reactions in living organi
2 CONC sms and in the test tube followed the same laws.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1904 he delivered at the University of California a course of lectures, the object of which was to illustrate the application of the methods of physical chemistry to the study of the theory of toxins and antitoxins, and which w
2 CONC ere published in 1907 under the title Immunochemistry. He also turned his attention to geology (the origin of ice ages), astronomy, physical cosmology, and astrophysics, accounting for the birth of the solar system by interstellar
2 CONC  collision. He considered radiation pressure as accounting for comets, the solar corona, the aurora borealis, and zodiacal light.
2 CONT
2 CONT He thought life might have been carried from planet to planet by the transport of spores, the theory now known as panspermia. He thought of the idea of a universal language, proposing a modification of the English language.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a board member for the Swedish Society for Racial Hygiene (founded 1909), which endorsed mendelism at the time, and contributed to the topic of contraceptives around 1910. However, until 1938 information and sale of contrac
2 CONC eptives was prohibited in the Kingdom of Sweden. Around 1930, conservative members of the society helped to establish eugenic policies in Sweden. Gordon Stein wrote that Svante Arrhenius was an atheist. In his last years he wrote 
2 CONC both textbooks and popular books, trying to emphasize the need for further work on the topics he discussed. In September 1927 he came down with an attack of acute intestinal catarrh and died on 2 October. He was buried in Uppsala.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriages and family''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married twice, first to his former pupil Sofia Rudbeck (1894 to 1896), with whom he had one son Olof Arrhenius, and then to Maria Johansson (1905 to 1927), with whom he had two daughters and a son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrhenius was the grandfather of bacteriologist Agnes Wold.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Greenhouse effect''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT In developing a theory to explain the ice ages, Arrhenius, in 1896, was the first to use basic principles of physical chemistry to calculate estimates of the extent to which increases in atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO2) will incre
2 CONC ase Earth's surface temperature through the greenhouse effect. These calculations led him to conclude that human-caused CO2 emissions, from fossil-fuel burning and other combustion processes, are large enough to cause global warmi
2 CONC ng. This conclusion has been extensively tested, winning a place at the core of modern climate science.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrhenius, in this work, built upon the prior work of other famous scientists, including Joseph Fourier, John Tyndall and Claude Pouillet. Arrhenius wanted to determine whether greenhouse gases could contribute to the explanation 
2 CONC of the temperature variation between glacial and inter-glacial periods.[26] Arrhenius used infrared observations of the moon – by Frank Washington Very and Samuel Pierpont Langley at the Allegheny Observatory in Pittsburgh — to ca
2 CONC lculate how much of infrared (heat) radiation is captured by CO2 and water (H2O) vapour in Earth's atmosphere. Using 'Stefan's law' (better known as the Stefan–Boltzmann law), he formulated what he referred to as a 'rule'
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://nobelprize.org/nobel_prizes/chemistry/laureates/1903/arrhenius-bio.html Nobelpris i Kemi 1903, läs hela biografin]
2 CONT
2 CONT
